From: Domenico D'Arrigo <DCHEM88@hotmail.it>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 20:06:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cpu/intel/haswell: Lock AES-NI enablement

SET_MSR_AESNI_LOCK_BIT defaults to y and promises to lock the AES-NI
enablement state, but the Haswell code never calls set_aesni_lock(), so
the lock bit in MSR 0x13c stays clear. Sandy Bridge and Ivy Bridge do
call it.

Call set_aesni_lock() from cpu_core_init(), which also runs on S3
resume.

TEST=Built for lenovo/w541. Not tested on hardware yet: rdmsr 0x13c
must report bit 0 set on all cores.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018n3yuWwDLW63F4vdf7ZFTv
---
 src/cpu/intel/haswell/haswell_init.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cpu/intel/haswell/haswell_init.c b/src/cpu/intel/haswell/haswell_init.c
index 499768159..9cfd94f60 100644
--- a/src/cpu/intel/haswell/haswell_init.c
+++ b/src/cpu/intel/haswell/haswell_init.c
@@ -498,6 +498,8 @@ static void cpu_core_init(struct device *cpu)
 	/* Set virtualization based on Kconfig option */
 	set_vmx_and_lock();
 
+	set_aesni_lock();
+
 	/* Configure C States */
 	configure_c_states();
 
